# device.env — what is plugged into THIS machine.
#
# You fill this in during lesson 00. The course's checks read it to find your debug
# console and to recognise your device on the host; without it they have nothing to
# open and nothing to look for.
#
# This file is machine-specific — the serial port path is different on your laptop, on
# your desktop, and after you unplug and replug — so it is in .gitignore and it is not
# committed. The template it came from is, so a fresh clone knows what to fill in.
#
# Format: KEY=value, one per line. No quotes, no spaces around the "=", no trailing
# comment on a value line. Lines starting with "#" and blank lines are ignored.


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CONSOLE_PORT — the serial port of your debug console.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# This is NOT the adapter Pico. It is the SECOND Pico, the one running `debugprobe`,
# which turns your firmware's UART0 output into a USB serial port on this machine.
# Plug that one in and find its port name:
#
#   Linux
#     ls -l /dev/serial/by-id/
#   You want the entry whose name mentions Debugprobe or CMSIS-DAP, for example
#     /dev/serial/by-id/usb-Raspberry_Pi_Debugprobe_on_Pico_CMSIS-DAP_E6614...-if00
#   Use that /dev/serial/by-id/... path rather than /dev/ttyACM0: the by-id name is
#   stable, and ttyACM numbering shifts as you plug things in.
#   If opening it gives "Permission denied", add yourself to the `dialout` group
#   (`sudo usermod -aG dialout $USER`) and log out and back in.
#
#   macOS
#     ls /dev/cu.usbmodem*
#   Use the cu.* name, NOT the matching tty.* name — opening a tty.* device waits for
#   a carrier-detect signal that a Pico never asserts, and the check will simply hang.
#   Example: /dev/cu.usbmodem1101
#
#   Windows
#     Device Manager -> Ports (COM & LPT), look for "USB Serial Device (COMn)",
#   or in PowerShell:
#     [System.IO.Ports.SerialPort]::GetPortNames()
#   Example: COM7
#
# The debugprobe presents TWO USB functions: a CMSIS-DAP debug interface and this
# serial port. Pick the serial one. If you see two candidate ports, unplug the probe,
# list them again, plug it back in, and take the one that appeared.
#
CONSOLE_PORT=


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CONSOLE_BAUD — the speed of that console.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 115200 bits per second, 8 data bits, no parity, 1 stop bit. This matches
# `debugprobe`'s own default, so leave it alone unless you deliberately change both
# ends. If your firmware and this value disagree you get a console full of plausible
# looking garbage rather than an error, so it is worth being sure.
#
CONSOLE_BAUD=115200


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# USB_VID / USB_PID — how the host will recognise your adapter.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Four hexadecimal digits each, lower case, with no leading "0x". These are the values
# you will put in the device descriptor you write by hand in lesson 11, and the values
# the host-side checks use to pick your device out of everything else plugged in.
#
# The defaults below are the pid.codes test pair 1209:0001, which exists precisely so
# that people developing USB devices do not have to invent a vendor ID. It is allocated
# for development and explicitly NOT for anything you distribute. Lesson 11 explains
# why making one up is not an option for a device that leaves your bench.
#
# Change these only if you have a reason to — and if you do, change them here and in
# your descriptor together, or the host checks will look for a device that is not there.
#
USB_VID=1209
USB_PID=0001


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# That is everything.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Nothing else belongs in this file. In particular:
#
#   - The build directory is not configurable. The build check runs
#       cmake -S . -B build
#     from the root of this repository and looks for what it produced under `build/`,
#     so build there.
#
#   - PICO_SDK_PATH belongs in your shell environment, not here. It says where the
#     SDK is installed on this machine, which is a property of the machine and not
#     of this project, and CMake reads it from the environment.
